    <section anchor="the-quarter-round">
      <name>The Quarter Round</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-QUARTER-ROUND">
        <t>After running a Quarter Round on these four numbers, we get these:
<xref section="2.1.1" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: the quarter round works on four 32-bit words, and std/crypto/chacha20poly1305.ts exports it only as chacha20QuarterRound over the <tt>u32[]</tt> state, which vector's types cannot express (they have <tt>u8[]</tt> and no other array); chacha20Block runs it, so the 2.3.2 and A.1 vectors test it</t>
      <blockquote anchor="R-QUARTER-ROUND-STATE">
        <t>We will apply the QUARTERROUND(2, 7, 8, 13) operation to this state.
<xref section="2.2.1" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: the vector works on the 16-word <tt>u32[]</tt> ChaCha state, which vector's types cannot express (they have <tt>u8[]</tt> and no other array); chacha20Block runs every quarter round on that state, so the 2.3.2 and A.1 vectors test it</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="the-chacha20-block-function">
      <name>The ChaCha20 Block Function</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-BLOCK">
        <t>The output is 64 random-looking bytes.
<xref section="2.3" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote>
        <t>Words 13-15 are a nonce, which <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> not be repeated for the same key.
<xref section="2.3" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: an obligation on the caller: a pure function cannot see its other invocations</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="the-chacha20-encryption-algorithm">
      <name>The ChaCha20 Encryption Algorithm</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-STREAM">
        <t>ChaCha20 successively calls the ChaCha20 block function, with the same
key and nonce, and with successively increasing block counter parameters.
ChaCha20 then serializes the resulting state by writing the numbers in
little-endian order, creating a keystream block. Concatenating the
keystream blocks from the successive blocks forms a keystream. The
ChaCha20 function then performs an XOR of this keystream with the
plaintext.
<xref section="2.4" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
    </section>
    <section anchor="the-poly1305-algorithm">
      <name>The Poly1305 Algorithm</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-POLY">
        <t>Poly1305 takes a 32-byte one-time key and a message and produces a 16-byte tag.
<xref section="2.5" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote>
        <t>The pair (r,s) should be unique, and <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> be unpredictable for each invocation (that is why it was originally obtained by encrypting a nonce), while "r" <bcp14>MAY</bcp14> be constant, but needs to be modified as follows before being used: ("r" is treated as a 16-octet little-endian number):
<xref section="2.5" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: the AEAD derives (r,s) per invocation from key and nonce (P-KEYGEN-IS-BLOCK0, P-TAG-IS-POLY1305); unpredictability is a property of the key, which no functional test observes</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="generating-the-poly1305-key-using-chacha20">
      <name>Generating the Poly1305 Key Using ChaCha20</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-KEYGEN">
        <t>We take the first 256 bits of the serialized state, and use those as the one- time Poly1305 key
<xref section="2.6" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote>
        <t>Any document that specifies the use of Poly1305 as a MAC algorithm for some protocol <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> specify that 256 bits are allocated for the integrity key.
<xref section="2.6" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: an obligation on protocol specifications, not on this module</t>
      <blockquote>
        <t>This <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> be unique per invocation with the same key, so it <bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14> be randomly generated.
<xref section="2.6" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: nonce management is the caller's</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="aead-construction">
      <name>AEAD Construction</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-OTK">
        <t>First, a Poly1305 one-time key is generated from the 256-bit key and nonce using the procedure described in Section 2.6.
<xref section="2.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-CTR1">
        <t>Next, the ChaCha20 encryption function is called to encrypt the plaintext, using the same key and nonce, and with the initial counter set to 1.
<xref section="2.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-PAD">
        <t>padding1 -- the padding is up to 15 zero bytes, and it brings the total length so far to an integral multiple of 16.
<xref section="2.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-MAC-INPUT">
        <t>The length of the additional data in octets (as a 64-bit little-endian integer). * The length of the ciphertext in octets (as a 64-bit little- endian integer).
<xref section="2.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-OUTPUT">
        <t>A ciphertext of the same length as the plaintext. o A 128-bit tag, which is the output of the Poly1305 function.
<xref section="2.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-DECRYPT">
        <t>The roles of ciphertext and plaintext are reversed, so the ChaCha20 encryption function is applied to the ciphertext, producing the plaintext.
<xref section="2.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-AUTH">
        <t>The calculated tag is bitwise compared to the received tag. The message is authenticated if and only if the tags match.
<xref section="2.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-K-LEN">
        <t>K_LEN (key length) is 32 octets.
<xref section="2.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-N-LEN">
        <t>N_MIN = N_MAX = 12 octets.
<xref section="2.8" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
    </section>
    <section anchor="security-considerations">
      <name>Security Considerations</name>
      <blockquote anchor="R-FULL-TAG">
        <t>Additionally, any protocol using this algorithm <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> include the complete tag to minimize the opportunity for forgery.
<xref section="4" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote anchor="R-NO-TRUNCATION">
        <t>Tag truncation <bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14> be done.
<xref section="4" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <blockquote>
        <t>The Poly1305 key <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> be unpredictable to an attacker.
<xref section="4" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: key management is the caller's</t>
      <blockquote>
        <t>For this reason, with online protocols, implementation <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> use a constant-time comparison function rather than relying on optimized but insecure library functions such as the C language's memcmp().
<xref section="4" sectionFormat="of" target="RFC8439"/></t>
      </blockquote>
      <t>Untested: timing is not observable by a functional test; nish checks it on the emitted machine code (tests/cases/ct_asm_chacha20poly1305.ts, docs/security/crypto-aead.md)</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="out-of-scope">
      <name>Out of Scope</name>
      <t>Out of scope: chacha20QuarterRound, it takes and returns the ChaCha20 state as <tt>u32[]</tt>, which the spec's types cannot express; chacha20Block runs every quarter round, so the 2.3.2 and A.1 vectors test its effect</t>
      <t>Out of scope: chacha20HeaderMask, QUIC header protection, specified by RFC 9001, not by RFC 8439</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="properties">
      <name>Properties</name>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-BLOCK-64 covers R-BLOCK — the block function outputs 64 bytes for any counter
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, counter: i32 /* 0..2147483647 */) =>
  unwrap(chacha20Block(key, counter, nonce)).length === 64
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-KEYSTREAM covers R-STREAM — ChaCha20 of zeros is the blocks for counter, counter+1 serialized end to end, cut to length
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, counter: i32 /* 0..100000 */, n: i32 /* 0..128 */) =>
  unwrap(chacha20(key, counter, nonce, zeros(n))) === unwrap(chacha20Block(key, counter, nonce)).concat(unwrap(chacha20Block(key, counter + 1, nonce))).slice(0, n)
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-XOR-INVOLUTION covers R-STREAM — ChaCha20 is an XOR stream: applying it twice gives the plaintext back, length kept
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, counter: i32 /* 0..100000 */, pt: u8[] /* 0..300 */) =>
  unwrap(chacha20(key, counter, nonce, pt)).length === pt.length && unwrap(chacha20(key, counter, nonce, unwrap(chacha20(key, counter, nonce, pt)))) === pt
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-POLY-16 covers R-POLY — Poly1305 takes a 32-byte key and any message and gives a 16-byte tag
forall (otk: u8[] /* 32..32 */, msg: u8[] /* 0..300 */) =>
  unwrap(poly1305(otk, msg)).length === 16
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-KEYGEN-IS-BLOCK0 covers R-KEYGEN — the one-time key is the first 32 bytes of block 0
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */) =>
  poly1305KeyGen(key, nonce) === unwrap(chacha20Block(key, 0, nonce)).slice(0, 32)
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-SEAL-SHAPE covers R-OUTPUT, R-FULL-TAG — the sealed output is a ciphertext as long as the plaintext plus a 16-byte tag
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, aad: u8[] /* 0..80 */, pt: u8[] /* 0..300 */) =>
  unwrap(chacha20Poly1305Seal(key, nonce, aad, pt)).length === pt.length + 16
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-CT-IS-CHACHA20 covers R-CTR1 — the ciphertext is ChaCha20 under the same key and nonce from counter 1
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, aad: u8[] /* 0..40 */, pt: u8[] /* 0..300 */) =>
  unwrap(chacha20Poly1305Seal(key, nonce, aad, pt)).slice(0, pt.length) === unwrap(chacha20(key, 1, nonce, pt))
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-TAG-IS-POLY1305 covers R-OTK, R-MAC-INPUT, R-PAD — the tag is Poly1305 under the one-time key over AAD, pad, ciphertext, pad and both lengths
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, aad: u8[] /* 0..80 */, pt: u8[] /* 0..300 */) =>
  { const sealed = unwrap(chacha20Poly1305Seal(key, nonce, aad, pt)); const ct = sealed.slice(0, pt.length); return sealed.slice(pt.length, pt.length + 16) === unwrap(poly1305(unwrap(poly1305KeyGen(key, nonce)), aad.concat(zeros((16 - aad.length % 16) % 16)).concat(ct).concat(zeros((16 - ct.length % 16) % 16)).concat(le64(aad.length)).concat(le64(pt.length)))); }
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-ROUNDTRIP covers R-DECRYPT — opening what was sealed gives the plaintext back
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, aad: u8[] /* 0..80 */, pt: u8[] /* 0..300 */) =>
  chacha20Poly1305Open(key, nonce, aad, unwrap(chacha20Poly1305Seal(key, nonce, aad, pt))) === pt
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-TAMPER-SEALED covers R-AUTH — flipping any one bit of the ciphertext or tag is refused
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, aad: u8[] /* 0..40 */, pt: u8[] /* 0..100 */, bit: i32 /* 0..927 */) =>
  chacha20Poly1305Open(key, nonce, aad, flipBit(unwrap(chacha20Poly1305Seal(key, nonce, aad, pt)), bit)) === null
where bit < 8 * (pt.length + 16)
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-TAMPER-AAD covers R-AUTH — flipping any one bit of the AAD is refused
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, aad: u8[] /* 1..40 */, pt: u8[] /* 0..100 */, bit: i32 /* 0..319 */) =>
  chacha20Poly1305Open(key, nonce, flipBit(aad, bit), unwrap(chacha20Poly1305Seal(key, nonce, aad, pt))) === null
where bit < 8 * aad.length
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-NO-TRUNCATION covers R-NO-TRUNCATION, R-FULL-TAG — a sealed message missing any part of its tag is refused
forall (key: u8[] /* 32..32 */, nonce: u8[] /* 12..12 */, aad: u8[] /* 0..40 */, pt: u8[] /* 0..100 */, cut: i32 /* 1..16 */) =>
  { const sealed = unwrap(chacha20Poly1305Seal(key, nonce, aad, pt)); return chacha20Poly1305Open(key, nonce, aad, sealed.slice(0, sealed.length - cut)) === null; }
]]></sourcecode>
      <sourcecode type="ts-property"><![CDATA[
// P-SIZES covers R-K-LEN, R-N-LEN — a key other than 32 octets or a nonce other than 12 is refused
forall (key: u8[] /* 0..40 */, nonce: u8[] /* 0..16 */) =>
  (key.length === 32 && nonce.length === 12) === (chacha20Poly1305Seal(key, nonce, [], []) !== null)
]]></sourcecode>
    </section>
